  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nReturn flow will develop Thursday night as the high pressure system \nmoves into the Ohio Valley while Lee side cyclogenesis begins to \noccur over Wyoming and Colorado. NBM output for PoPs Thursday night \nhave been inconsistent. Most likely, there will be a few showers and \nisolated thunderstorms around. Proximity sounding sampling of \nnumerous deterministic models suggest saturation will again be a \nchallenge but even shallow saturation in optimal locations in the \nprofile will lead to free parcel lift and modest convective \ninstability. Friday and Saturday will feature an increasing pressure \ngradient across the Midwest leading to breezy to gusty conditions on \nFriday. Windy conditions are possible on Saturday with the \ncombination of the pressure gradient approaching 30 ubars/km over \nthe northwest and mixed layer winds near 40 kts. Temperature \nadvection through the profile will be near neutral so mixing \nefficiency will be average but not as efficient as with cold to \nstrong cold advection. As discussed yesterday, an upper low \ndescending into the Pacific Northwest this weekend will bring a \ntransition from westerly to southwesterly flow to Iowa late weekend \nand into early next week. The extended forecast beyond that is quite \nnoisy with below average handling of this upper low. The impacts \nfrom another upper low lifting out of the south central CONUS and \ningested tropical system this weekend appears to remain to the \nsoutheast of the forecast area.",
